<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mikhail of Chernigov</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1185–1246</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Michael of Chernigov</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mikhail of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a> was prince of Chernigov and, at times, grand prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> in the years of the Mongol invasion. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> sacked Chernigov in 1239 he fled abroad, and in 1246 he was executed at the camp of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a>, reportedly for refusing a pagan purification rite. He is venerated as a martyr, and his sons founded the princely lines of the Chernigov lands, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bryansk">Bryansk</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mikhail-of-chernigov.jpg" alt="Mikhail of Chernigov" width="320" height="322" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saints_Boyar_Theodore_and_Prince_Michael_of_Chernigov.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
